No. Retrying with a stale `desired` is not a correct increment: a successful compare-and-exchange can write the current value back unchanged, losing an increment.

For a concrete execution, start with the counter at 0:
1. Threads A and B both load `expected = 0` and compute `desired = 1`.
2. A successfully compares against 0 and writes 1; A finishes its increment.
3. B compares against 0 and fails because the counter is now 1. Its `expected` becomes 1, but its `desired` remains 1.
4. B retries, successfully compares against 1, and writes 1; B also finishes. Two increments completed, but the final count is 1 instead of 2. No spurious failure is needed for this execution.

The fix is to recompute `desired` before every attempt, using the latest `expected`. A spurious failure also returns to that recomputation, so it cannot cause an incorrect increment; as usual, termination requires that attempts eventually succeed. Relaxed ordering suffices for this counter alone, and joining all worker threads lets the test check the completed total. The unsigned counter wraps modulo its range; this test stays well below that limit.

```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

void increment(std::atomic<std::uint64_t>& counter) {
    std::uint64_t expected = counter.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
    for (;;) {
        const std::uint64_t desired = expected + std::uint64_t{1};
        if (counter.compare_exchange_weak(
                expected, desired,
                std::memory_order_relaxed,
                std::memory_order_relaxed)) {
            return;
        }
        // Failure, including spurious failure, updates expected with
        // the observed value. Recompute desired on the next attempt.
    }
}

int main() {
    constexpr unsigned thread_count = 8;
    constexpr std::uint64_t increments_per_thread = 100000;
    constexpr std::uint64_t expected_total =
        std::uint64_t{thread_count} * increments_per_thread;

    std::atomic<std::uint64_t> counter{0};
    std::vector<std::thread> workers;
    workers.reserve(thread_count);

    for (unsigned i = 0; i < thread_count; ++i) {
        workers.emplace_back([&counter] {
            for (std::uint64_t j = 0; j < increments_per_thread; ++j) {
                increment(counter);
            }
        });
    }

    for (auto& worker : workers) {
        worker.join();
    }

    const std::uint64_t actual = counter.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
    if (actual != expected_total) {
        std::cerr << "FAIL: expected " << expected_total
                  << ", got " << actual << '\n';
        return 1;
    }

    std::cout << "PASS: final count = " << actual << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

